Let $E$ denote the given expression. Apply $\sin x\cos x=\frac{1}{2}\sin(2x)$ at $x=78.75^\circ$. Dividing by secant multiplies by cosine because $1/\sec x=\cos x$. The resulting pair at $x=157.5^\circ$ permits a second application.
$$
\begin{aligned}
E&=\frac{\sin\left(157.5^\circ\right)}{\sec\left(157.5^\circ\right)}\\&=\sin\left(157.5^\circ\right)\cos\left(157.5^\circ\right)\\&=\frac{1}{2}\,\sin\left(315^\circ\right)
\end{aligned}
$$
The terminal sine is $\sin\left(315^\circ\right)=- \frac{\sqrt{2}}{2}$.
Therefore,
$$
\begin{aligned}
E&=\frac{1}{2}\,\left(- \frac{\sqrt{2}}{2}\right)\\&=- \frac{\sqrt{2}}{4}.
\end{aligned}
$$

Let $E$ denote the given expression. Apply $\sin x\cos x=\frac{1}{2}\sin(2x)$ at $x=\frac{7 \pi}{24}$ in the denominator. Use $\sec x=1/\cos x$, then apply the double-angle identity again at $x=\frac{7 \pi}{12}$.
$$
\begin{aligned}
E&=\frac{4\,\sec\left(\frac{7 \pi}{12}\right)}{\sin\left(\frac{7 \pi}{12}\right)}\\&=\frac{4}{\sin\left(\frac{7 \pi}{12}\right)\cos\left(\frac{7 \pi}{12}\right)}\\&=\frac{8}{\sin\left(\frac{7 \pi}{6}\right)}
\end{aligned}
$$
The terminal sine is $\sin\left(\frac{7 \pi}{6}\right)=- \frac{1}{2}$.
Therefore,
$$
\begin{aligned}
E&=\frac{8}{\left(- \frac{1}{2}\right)}\\&=-16.
\end{aligned}
$$

Let $E$ denote the given expression. Apply $\sin x\cos x=\frac{1}{2}\sin(2x)$ at $x=\frac{\pi}{24}$ in the denominator. Use $\sec x=1/\cos x$, then apply the double-angle identity again at $x=\frac{\pi}{12}$.
$$
\begin{aligned}
E&=\frac{2\,\sec\left(\frac{\pi}{12}\right)}{\sin\left(\frac{\pi}{12}\right)}\\&=\frac{2}{\sin\left(\frac{\pi}{12}\right)\cos\left(\frac{\pi}{12}\right)}\\&=\frac{4}{\sin\left(\frac{\pi}{6}\right)}
\end{aligned}
$$
The terminal sine is $\sin\left(\frac{\pi}{6}\right)=\frac{1}{2}$.
Therefore,
$$
\begin{aligned}
E&=\frac{4}{\left(\frac{1}{2}\right)}\\&=8.
\end{aligned}
$$

Let $E$ denote the given expression. Apply $\sin x\cos x=\frac{1}{2}\sin(2x)$ at $x=\frac{11 \pi}{24}$. Dividing by secant multiplies by cosine because $1/\sec x=\cos x$. The resulting pair at $x=\frac{11 \pi}{12}$ permits a second application.
$$
\begin{aligned}
E&=\frac{\frac{1}{2}\,\sin\left(\frac{11 \pi}{12}\right)}{\sec\left(\frac{11 \pi}{12}\right)}\\&=\frac{1}{2}\,\sin\left(\frac{11 \pi}{12}\right)\cos\left(\frac{11 \pi}{12}\right)\\&=\frac{1}{4}\,\sin\left(\frac{11 \pi}{6}\right)
\end{aligned}
$$
The terminal sine is $\sin\left(\frac{11 \pi}{6}\right)=- \frac{1}{2}$.
Therefore,
$$
\begin{aligned}
E&=\frac{1}{4}\,\left(- \frac{1}{2}\right)\\&=- \frac{1}{8}.
\end{aligned}
$$

Let $E$ denote the given expression. Apply $\sin x\cos x=\frac{1}{2}\sin(2x)$ at $x=37.5^\circ$ in the denominator. Use $\sec x=1/\cos x$, then apply the double-angle identity again at $x=75^\circ$.
$$
\begin{aligned}
E&=\frac{4\,\sec\left(75^\circ\right)}{\sin\left(75^\circ\right)}\\&=\frac{4}{\sin\left(75^\circ\right)\cos\left(75^\circ\right)}\\&=\frac{8}{\sin\left(150^\circ\right)}
\end{aligned}
$$
The terminal sine is $\sin\left(150^\circ\right)=\frac{1}{2}$.
Therefore,
$$
\begin{aligned}
E&=\frac{8}{\left(\frac{1}{2}\right)}\\&=16.
\end{aligned}
$$
